window.READING_PRACTICE_BANK = {"schema_version":"3.5","bank_id":"READING_PRACTICE_10_TOPICS_V3_5","title":"Reading Practice · 10 thematic topics","provenance":"GENERATED_PRACTICE_FROM_PRIOR_APP","source_note":"Imported from Aptis_Study_Hub_V3_2_Mixed_Review / Original Day 1–Day 5 thematic banks. This is practice content, not teacher-source/canonical content.","items":[{"id":"T01__rpart1_0__fh_r1_1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"beside"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"through"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Mai,\nThanks for agreeing to visit us on Saturday. We have recently moved into a new flat, so please come to the building (1) ___ the small supermarket. Our apartment is (2) ___ the third floor. We are planning to have lunch at about 12:30, and my mother will (3) ___ a simple meal. If the weather is good, we may sit on the balcony because it is more (4) ___ than the dining room. Please call me if you have any trouble finding the place. I am really looking forward to (5) ___ you.\nLan","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart1_1__fh_r1_2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"at"},{"id":"b","text":"on"},{"id":"c","text":"into"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Mai,\nThanks for agreeing to visit us on Saturday. We have recently moved into a new flat, so please come to the building (1) ___ the small supermarket. Our apartment is (2) ___ the third floor. We are planning to have lunch at about 12:30, and my mother will (3) ___ a simple meal. If the weather is good, we may sit on the balcony because it is more (4) ___ than the dining room. Please call me if you have any trouble finding the place. I am really looking forward to (5) ___ you.\nLan","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart1_2__fh_r1_3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"cook"},{"id":"b","text":"travel"},{"id":"c","text":"borrow"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Mai,\nThanks for agreeing to visit us on Saturday. We have recently moved into a new flat, so please come to the building (1) ___ the small supermarket. Our apartment is (2) ___ the third floor. We are planning to have lunch at about 12:30, and my mother will (3) ___ a simple meal. If the weather is good, we may sit on the balcony because it is more (4) ___ than the dining room. Please call me if you have any trouble finding the place. I am really looking forward to (5) ___ you.\nLan","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart1_3__fh_r1_4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"spacious"},{"id":"b","text":"delayed"},{"id":"c","text":"formal"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Mai,\nThanks for agreeing to visit us on Saturday. We have recently moved into a new flat, so please come to the building (1) ___ the small supermarket. Our apartment is (2) ___ the third floor. We are planning to have lunch at about 12:30, and my mother will (3) ___ a simple meal. If the weather is good, we may sit on the balcony because it is more (4) ___ than the dining room. Please call me if you have any trouble finding the place. I am really looking forward to (5) ___ you.\nLan","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart1_4__fh_r1_5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"see"},{"id":"b","text":"seeing"},{"id":"c","text":"saw"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Mai,\nThanks for agreeing to visit us on Saturday. We have recently moved into a new flat, so please come to the building (1) ___ the small supermarket. Our apartment is (2) ___ the third floor. We are planning to have lunch at about 12:30, and my mother will (3) ___ a simple meal. If the weather is good, we may sit on the balcony because it is more (4) ___ than the dining room. Please call me if you have any trouble finding the place. I am really looking forward to (5) ___ you.\nLan","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart2_0__fh_r2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Order the five sentences.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Last month, my family decided to change the way we shared household chores.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"Before that, my mother had been doing most of the cleaning and cooking herself."},{"id":"s2","text":"We therefore made a simple weekly list and gave each person two regular jobs."},{"id":"s3","text":"At first, my younger brother complained because he did not want to clean the bathroom."},{"id":"s4","text":"After a few weeks, however, everyone became used to the new routine."},{"id":"s5","text":"The house is now tidier, and my mother has more time to relax in the evenings."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart2_1__fh_r2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Order the five sentences.","context":"Fixed first sentence: When my grandparents moved to a smaller flat, they had to decide what furniture to keep.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"Their old house contained several large pieces that would not fit into the new rooms."},{"id":"s2","text":"Instead of throwing everything away, they invited the family to choose useful items."},{"id":"s3","text":"My sister took a small desk, while I chose an old wooden bookshelf."},{"id":"s4","text":"The remaining furniture was donated to a local charity that helps families moving into empty homes."},{"id":"s5","text":"In the end, the move felt less sad because many of their possessions continued to be useful."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart4_0__fh_r4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Prefers convenience to a large home."},{"id":"p2","text":"Needs private space even though family matters."},{"id":"p3","text":"Likes relatives living close enough for informal visits."},{"id":"p4","text":"Values objects with personal meaning."},{"id":"p5","text":"Believes shared meals support relationships."},{"id":"p6","text":"Prefers fewer but more focused family meetings."},{"id":"p7","text":"Thinks traditions help younger family members understand their background."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"c","p2":"b","p3":"a","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"b","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I grew up in a busy household, so I enjoy having people around me. I do not mind a little noise, and I think shared meals are one of the best ways to keep family relationships strong. I also like living near relatives because it makes spontaneous visits possible.\n\nPerson B — I love my family, but I need plenty of personal space. I would rather meet relatives once a week and have a proper conversation than see them every day. At home, I value a quiet room where I can read or work without interruption.\n\nPerson C — For me, a good home is mainly practical. I prefer a small flat near public transport to a large house far from work. I do not spend much money on decoration because convenience matters more to me than appearance.\n\nPerson D — I enjoy making a home feel personal. I like photographs, plants and furniture with a story behind it. I also think family traditions are important because they give children a sense of where they come from, even if everyone is busy.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T01__rpart5_0__fh_r5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"Changing ideas about what makes a successful home"},{"id":"h2","text":"Using the same space for different purposes"},{"id":"h3","text":"The mixed effect of digital technology on family interaction"},{"id":"h4","text":"Making household responsibilities clearer"},{"id":"h5","text":"Why personal comfort is not the same as perfect design"},{"id":"h6","text":"The influence of the area outside the home"},{"id":"h7","text":"No single definition fits every household"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why families should always choose the largest house available"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nFor much of the twentieth century, the idea of a successful home was strongly connected with size and ownership. A larger house was often treated as a sign that a family had achieved stability. In many cities today, however, rising property prices and changing lifestyles are challenging that assumption. Young adults may move frequently for study or work, older people may prefer smaller homes that are easier to maintain, and some families deliberately choose compact apartments in central areas. As a result, researchers are paying more attention to how people use space rather than simply how much space they have.\n\nParagraph B\nOne important change is the growing interest in flexible rooms. In the past, each room often had a single purpose: a dining room was for meals, a study was for work and a bedroom was for sleeping. Modern households may not have enough space for that separation. A table may be used for breakfast in the morning, online work in the afternoon and homework in the evening. Furniture that can be moved, folded or stored therefore becomes valuable. This flexibility can make a small home function surprisingly well, although it requires family members to communicate about when and how shared areas will be used.\n\nParagraph C\nTechnology has also altered family life at home. Video calls allow relatives in different cities or countries to remain connected, while online entertainment means that several people can spend an evening in the same room while concentrating on different screens. This creates a strange combination of physical closeness and individual activity. Some families respond by creating periods when phones and laptops are put aside, particularly during meals. Others are comfortable with parallel activities and do not believe that being together always requires direct conversation. The important question may therefore be not how much technology a household uses, but whether its use supports or interrupts the relationships people want to maintain.\n\nParagraph D\nAnother trend concerns the division of household work. Surveys repeatedly show that arguments about cleaning, cooking and childcare can create stress even when the tasks themselves are not difficult. The problem is often that responsibility is unclear or feels unfair. Some households have introduced written schedules, shared apps or weekly meetings to make duties more visible. These systems can feel unnecessary at first, but supporters say they reduce the need for repeated reminders. Critics argue that family life should not become a management project. Even so, most agree that discussing expectations openly is better than allowing frustration to build silently.\n\nParagraph E\nThe design of a home can also influence well-being. Natural light, ventilation and access to quiet areas affect concentration and mood, while clutter can make some people feel mentally overloaded. This does not mean that every home must follow a fashionable design style. In fact, personal objects may be more important than expensive decoration. Photographs, books, plants and furniture connected with family memories can make a space feel secure and familiar. A home that looks perfect in a magazine may be less satisfying than one that reflects the people who actually live there.\n\nParagraph F\nRelationships with the surrounding neighbourhood matter as well. A household does not exist in isolation. Nearby shops, parks, schools and transport links shape daily routines, and friendly neighbours can provide practical support. This became especially clear during periods when people spent more time close to home and relied on local networks. At the same time, not everyone wants a highly social neighbourhood. Some residents prefer privacy and limited contact. Successful communities therefore need to provide opportunities for connection without creating pressure to participate in every local activity.\n\nParagraph G\nTaken together, these changes suggest that there is no single model of the ideal home. For one person, comfort may mean a quiet apartment with strong privacy; for another, it may mean a busy house where relatives visit frequently. What appears to matter most is the match between the space, the routines and the people using them. Homes work well when they support daily needs, allow relationships to develop and can adapt as circumstances change. The modern definition of a good home is therefore becoming less about following one standard and more about creating an environment that is practical, meaningful and sustainable for the people who live there.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 1 · Family & Home"},{"id":"T02__rpart1_0__tt_r1_1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"at"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"into"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nHere are the details for our trip tomorrow. Please meet me (1) ___ the main entrance of the station at 7:20. Our train leaves at 7:45, so we should have enough time to find the correct (2) ___. I have already (3) ___ the tickets online. Remember to bring a light jacket because the weather may be cooler at our (4) ___. We are only staying for one night, so try to travel (5) ___ and bring a small bag.\nSam","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart1_1__tt_r1_2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"platform"},{"id":"b","text":"ingredient"},{"id":"c","text":"neighbourhood"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nHere are the details for our trip tomorrow. Please meet me (1) ___ the main entrance of the station at 7:20. Our train leaves at 7:45, so we should have enough time to find the correct (2) ___. I have already (3) ___ the tickets online. Remember to bring a light jacket because the weather may be cooler at our (4) ___. We are only staying for one night, so try to travel (5) ___ and bring a small bag.\nSam","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart1_2__tt_r1_3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"booked"},{"id":"b","text":"cooked"},{"id":"c","text":"decorated"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nHere are the details for our trip tomorrow. Please meet me (1) ___ the main entrance of the station at 7:20. Our train leaves at 7:45, so we should have enough time to find the correct (2) ___. I have already (3) ___ the tickets online. Remember to bring a light jacket because the weather may be cooler at our (4) ___. We are only staying for one night, so try to travel (5) ___ and bring a small bag.\nSam","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart1_3__tt_r1_4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"destination"},{"id":"b","text":"household"},{"id":"c","text":"recipe"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nHere are the details for our trip tomorrow. Please meet me (1) ___ the main entrance of the station at 7:20. Our train leaves at 7:45, so we should have enough time to find the correct (2) ___. I have already (3) ___ the tickets online. Remember to bring a light jacket because the weather may be cooler at our (4) ___. We are only staying for one night, so try to travel (5) ___ and bring a small bag.\nSam","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart1_4__tt_r1_5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"light"},{"id":"b","text":"heavy"},{"id":"c","text":"formal"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nHere are the details for our trip tomorrow. Please meet me (1) ___ the main entrance of the station at 7:20. Our train leaves at 7:45, so we should have enough time to find the correct (2) ___. I have already (3) ___ the tickets online. Remember to bring a light jacket because the weather may be cooler at our (4) ___. We are only staying for one night, so try to travel (5) ___ and bring a small bag.\nSam","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart2_0__tt_r2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Order the five sentences.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Last summer, my friend and I decided to travel to Hue by train instead of flying.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"We booked a sleeper cabin because the journey would take most of the night."},{"id":"s2","text":"On the evening of departure, we arrived at the station early and bought some food for the trip."},{"id":"s3","text":"Once the train started moving, we talked for a while and watched the city lights disappear."},{"id":"s4","text":"Later, we slept surprisingly well and woke up as the train was passing through the countryside."},{"id":"s5","text":"By the time we reached Hue, we felt rested and agreed that the journey had been part of the holiday experience."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart2_1__tt_r2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Order the five sentences.","context":"Fixed first sentence: My city recently introduced a new bicycle-sharing system near several metro stations.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"At first, many commuters were unsure whether the service would be convenient enough to use regularly."},{"id":"s2","text":"However, the city added more cycle lanes and secure parking areas during the following months."},{"id":"s3","text":"As a result, people began using bicycles for the short distance between their homes and the metro."},{"id":"s4","text":"The system has not solved every traffic problem, but it has made some daily journeys easier."},{"id":"s5","text":"City planners are now considering expanding the service to neighbourhoods that currently have fewer transport options."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart4_0__tt_r4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Values speed on long-distance trips."},{"id":"p2","text":"Enjoys the journey and scenery."},{"id":"p3","text":"Mainly uses a bicycle for short urban travel."},{"id":"p4","text":"Believes people should not need a private car."},{"id":"p5","text":"Prefers to carry very little luggage."},{"id":"p6","text":"Accepts a longer journey for a calmer experience."},{"id":"p7","text":"Uses public transport particularly when cycling is difficult."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"b","p7":"c"},"context":"Person A — I travel frequently for work, so speed matters most to me. I usually fly when the distance is long because I want to spend as little time as possible in transit. I do not mind airports, although I always try to travel with only one small bag.\n\nPerson B — I prefer trains whenever they are practical. I like being able to move around, read and look at the scenery. The journey feels less stressful than flying, even if it takes longer.\n\nPerson C — I rarely travel far, but in my city I use a bicycle for most short journeys. It is cheap, flexible and avoids parking problems. I only use public transport when the weather is very bad.\n\nPerson D — I do not drive, so reliable buses and metro services are essential for me. I think public transport should be affordable and frequent enough that people do not feel forced to own a car.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T02__rpart5_0__tt_r5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"Moving beyond transport systems designed mainly for cars"},{"id":"h2","text":"Why reliability can matter as much as price"},{"id":"h3","text":"Making separate forms of transport work as one network"},{"id":"h4","text":"What cycling needs in order to become a normal daily option"},{"id":"h5","text":"Managing public resistance to changes in road use"},{"id":"h6","text":"The limits of technology as a complete solution"},{"id":"h7","text":"Access and choice as the real measures of success"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why every city should ban private cars immediately"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nFor decades, urban transport planning concentrated mainly on moving cars efficiently. Wide roads, large junctions and parking areas were treated as signs of modern development. That approach is now being questioned in many cities. Population growth has increased congestion, while concerns about air quality and climate change have made heavy dependence on private vehicles more difficult to defend. Planners are therefore looking for systems that move large numbers of people while using less space and producing fewer emissions. The result is not one universal solution but a combination of rail, buses, walking, cycling and carefully managed road use.\n\nParagraph B\nPublic transport succeeds only when people can depend on it. A bus may be inexpensive, but commuters will avoid it if it arrives unpredictably or becomes so crowded that passengers cannot board. Frequency is particularly important because long waiting times make short journeys feel inefficient. Reliable information also matters. Real-time displays and journey-planning apps reduce uncertainty and allow passengers to adjust when disruptions occur. These features may appear less dramatic than building a new metro line, but improvements to reliability can change behaviour because they make public transport feel like a practical everyday option rather than a service used only when no alternative exists.\n\nParagraph C\nThe connection between different transport modes is another major issue. A commuter's journey may involve walking to a bus stop, taking a bus to a station and then using a train. If each part is planned separately, a small delay can make the whole trip inconvenient. Integrated ticketing and well-designed interchange points reduce this problem. Some cities now allow one payment method to be used across buses, trains and bicycle-sharing systems. This makes it easier for travellers to choose the most suitable combination rather than treating each transport mode as a separate system.\n\nParagraph D\nCycling has received particular attention because it is inexpensive, produces no direct emissions and requires relatively little road space. Yet simply encouraging people to cycle is not enough. Many potential cyclists are concerned about safety, especially when they must share busy roads with fast traffic. Protected lanes, secure parking and safe junction design can make a substantial difference. The most successful programmes also connect cycle routes with schools, workplaces and public transport stations, turning bicycles into a realistic part of daily mobility rather than only a recreational activity.\n\nParagraph E\nEfforts to reduce car use can be politically difficult. Drivers may fear that fewer parking spaces or narrower roads will make daily life harder, while local businesses sometimes worry that customers will go elsewhere. Evidence from pedestrian-friendly areas suggests that these concerns do not always become reality. Streets with less traffic can attract more people on foot, and visitors may spend longer in local shops and cafés. However, planners need to communicate clearly and introduce changes carefully. If alternatives are not available before restrictions are imposed, residents may reasonably feel that they are being punished rather than supported.\n\nParagraph F\nTechnology is likely to influence transport further, but predictions should be treated cautiously. Electric vehicles can reduce local air pollution, and automated systems may improve efficiency in some situations. Nevertheless, replacing every conventional car with an electric one would not solve congestion or the large amount of urban space required for parking. Digital tools are most useful when they help people use the wider transport network more effectively, for example by combining timetables, payment and route information in a single service. Technology can support good planning, but it cannot replace it.\n\nParagraph G\nThe broader lesson is that transport policy should be judged by access rather than by the speed of one particular vehicle. A successful system allows people of different ages and incomes to reach work, education, shops and social activities safely and reasonably quickly. That may require different solutions in different neighbourhoods. Dense central areas can support frequent public transport, while outer districts may need flexible buses or better connections to main routes. The goal is not to eliminate one form of travel completely. It is to create a network in which people have realistic choices and do not depend on a private car for every journey.\n\nAdditional note\nThis focus on choice also changes how success is measured. A transport system may be effective even when journey times are not dramatically shorter, provided people can travel more reliably, safely and affordably. For many residents, having several dependable options is itself a major improvement.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 1 · Travel & Transport"},{"id":"T03__rpart1_0__sr1_1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"at"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"through"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nOur outdoor club is meeting on Sunday. Please arrive (1) ___ the park entrance at 7:30. We will start with a short (2) ___ to prepare our muscles before the run. The route is about five kilometres and is not too (3) ___. Remember to bring water because the weather may be (4) ___. After the run, we are planning to have breakfast (5) ___ at a café near the lake.\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart1_1__sr1_2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"warm-up"},{"id":"b","text":"fare"},{"id":"c","text":"booking"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nOur outdoor club is meeting on Sunday. Please arrive (1) ___ the park entrance at 7:30. We will start with a short (2) ___ to prepare our muscles before the run. The route is about five kilometres and is not too (3) ___. Remember to bring water because the weather may be (4) ___. After the run, we are planning to have breakfast (5) ___ at a café near the lake.\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart1_2__sr1_3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"challenging"},{"id":"b","text":"spicy"},{"id":"c","text":"crowdedly"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nOur outdoor club is meeting on Sunday. Please arrive (1) ___ the park entrance at 7:30. We will start with a short (2) ___ to prepare our muscles before the run. The route is about five kilometres and is not too (3) ___. Remember to bring water because the weather may be (4) ___. After the run, we are planning to have breakfast (5) ___ at a café near the lake.\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart1_3__sr1_4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"hot"},{"id":"b","text":"formal"},{"id":"c","text":"indoor"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nOur outdoor club is meeting on Sunday. Please arrive (1) ___ the park entrance at 7:30. We will start with a short (2) ___ to prepare our muscles before the run. The route is about five kilometres and is not too (3) ___. Remember to bring water because the weather may be (4) ___. After the run, we are planning to have breakfast (5) ___ at a café near the lake.\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart1_4__sr1_5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"together"},{"id":"b","text":"careful"},{"id":"c","text":"late"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nOur outdoor club is meeting on Sunday. Please arrive (1) ___ the park entrance at 7:30. We will start with a short (2) ___ to prepare our muscles before the run. The route is about five kilometres and is not too (3) ___. Remember to bring water because the weather may be (4) ___. After the run, we are planning to have breakfast (5) ___ at a café near the lake.\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart2_0__sr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Last spring, I decided to prepare for my first five-kilometre race.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"At the beginning, I could only run for ten minutes before needing to walk."},{"id":"s2","text":"I therefore followed a simple plan that increased the distance gradually each week."},{"id":"s3","text":"After a month, I noticed that my breathing and confidence had both improved."},{"id":"s4","text":"I then practised the full distance twice before race day."},{"id":"s5","text":"When I finally crossed the finish line, I was tired but proud that the gradual training had worked."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart2_1__sr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our university wanted more students to use the outdoor sports area.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"A survey first showed that many students did not know what facilities were available."},{"id":"s2","text":"The university then organised free beginner sessions for football, running and fitness."},{"id":"s3","text":"It also installed better lighting so that the area could be used safely in the evening."},{"id":"s4","text":"Within several weeks, attendance increased and new informal sports groups appeared."},{"id":"s5","text":"The university is now considering adding more lockers and drinking-water points because the area is used much more often."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart4_0__sr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person enjoys competition and making friends through sport."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person chooses calm exercise partly to reduce stress."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person particularly values being outdoors."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person needs exercise to fit easily around a busy schedule."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person prefers activities without a competitive atmosphere."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person likes discovering new places while exercising."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person sometimes uses online instruction at home."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"b","p6":"c","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I enjoy team sports because other people make me push myself harder. I like competition, but the social side is equally important. I have made several close friends through football.\n\nPerson B — I exercise mainly to manage stress. I prefer yoga and long walks because I do not want exercise to feel like another competition. Quiet activities help me concentrate better afterwards.\n\nPerson C — I spend enough time indoors at work, so I choose activities that take me outside. Hiking and cycling are my favourites because I can explore different places while being active.\n\nPerson D — I am very busy, so convenience matters most. I do short workouts at home because I can exercise at any time without travelling to a gym. I sometimes follow online classes when I need more variety.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T03__rpart5_0__sr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"Why understanding the benefits is not enough"},{"id":"h2","text":"The problem with doing too much too soon"},{"id":"h3","text":"Enjoyment as part of an effective exercise plan"},{"id":"h4","text":"How other people can support or hinder a routine"},{"id":"h5","text":"Why outdoor environments matter for public health"},{"id":"h6","text":"The neglected role of recovery"},{"id":"h7","text":"A more realistic definition of exercise success"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why competitive sport should replace all other activity"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nRegular exercise is often described as a simple requirement for good health, yet knowing that activity is useful does not automatically make people active. Modern routines can involve long hours sitting at desks, travelling by car and using screens for entertainment. As a result, the main challenge for many adults is not understanding the benefits of exercise but finding a form of activity that can survive busy weeks, bad weather and changes in motivation. Researchers increasingly focus on habit design rather than short periods of extreme effort because long-term consistency appears to produce more reliable benefits.\n\nParagraph B\nOne common mistake is starting with a plan that is too demanding. A beginner may decide to run every day or spend an hour in the gym after years of little activity. The first sessions can feel exciting, but tiredness and muscle pain quickly make the routine difficult to maintain. Gradual programmes are less dramatic, yet they allow the body to adapt and give people repeated experiences of success. Someone who begins with twenty-minute walks may later add jogging or strength exercises without feeling that every session is a test of willpower.\n\nParagraph C\nEnjoyment is another powerful factor. Exercise chosen only because it is considered healthy may disappear as soon as motivation falls. People who dislike gyms may be far more consistent when they cycle, dance, swim or join a recreational sports group. This explains why experts hesitate to recommend one ideal activity for everyone. The physical benefits of different forms of exercise vary, but a theoretically perfect programme has little value if the person stops doing it after two weeks. Preference therefore becomes part of an effective health strategy rather than a trivial detail.\n\nParagraph D\nThe social environment can also influence whether an activity becomes a habit. Training with a friend or team can create accountability because another person expects you to arrive. Social groups can make difficult sessions more enjoyable and provide practical advice to beginners. However, social exercise is not suitable for everybody. Some people value quiet time and become more relaxed when they can walk or run alone. The useful principle is not that exercise must be social, but that the environment should support the individual's reasons for being active.\n\nParagraph E\nOutdoor activity has received particular attention because it combines movement with exposure to natural surroundings. Parks, riverside paths and hiking trails can make exercise feel less repetitive, and some studies associate time in green spaces with improved mood. Yet outdoor exercise depends on safe public environments. Poor lighting, dangerous road crossings or a lack of paths can discourage people, especially older adults and beginners. Investment in accessible parks and walking or cycling routes can therefore be considered part of public-health policy rather than simply an improvement to appearance.\n\nParagraph F\nRecovery is often ignored by people who become enthusiastic about fitness. The body needs time to repair after demanding exercise, and insufficient recovery can reduce performance or increase the risk of injury. Rest does not necessarily mean complete inactivity; gentle walking, stretching and good sleep may support the recovery process. Experienced athletes plan recovery deliberately, but the same principle applies to ordinary exercisers. More training is not always better. A routine should contain enough challenge to create improvement and enough rest to make that improvement sustainable.\n\nParagraph G\nThese ideas lead to a broader definition of successful exercise. The best routine is not the one that looks most impressive on paper or produces the greatest exhaustion after a single session. It is the one that a person can repeat, adapt and continue as circumstances change. Goals can still be useful, whether they involve running a race, improving strength or simply feeling more energetic. However, those goals work best when supported by realistic schedules, enjoyable activities and recovery. In the long term, consistency and adaptability are usually more important than intensity alone.\n\nAdditional note\nAccess also affects whether good intentions become habits. People are more likely to walk, run or cycle when safe routes begin near their homes and when facilities are affordable. This means that personal motivation and public design interact. An individual can choose to be active, but the surrounding environment can make that choice easier or much harder to maintain over time.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities"},{"id":"T04__rpart1_0__fr1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"some"},{"id":"b","text":"much"},{"id":"c","text":"anyone"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI am cooking dinner tonight. Could you buy (1) ___ vegetables on your way home? We already have rice, but there is not (2) ___ milk left for the sauce. I plan to (3) ___ the vegetables with a little olive oil and garlic. Please arrive by seven because I would like us to eat (4) ___. If you have time, you can also choose a simple dessert from the bakery (5) ___ the supermarket.\nThanks,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart1_1__fr2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"many"},{"id":"b","text":"much"},{"id":"c","text":"few"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Anna,\nI am cooking dinner tonight. Could you buy (1) ___ vegetables on your way home? We already have rice, but there is not (2) ___ milk left for the sauce. I plan to (3) ___ the vegetables with a little olive oil and garlic. Please arrive by seven because I would like us to eat (4) ___. If you have time, you can also choose a simple dessert from the bakery (5) ___ the supermarket.\nThanks,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart1_2__fr3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"fry"},{"id":"b","text":"board"},{"id":"c","text":"score"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI am cooking dinner tonight. Could you buy (1) ___ vegetables on your way home? We already have rice, but there is not (2) ___ milk left for the sauce. I plan to (3) ___ the vegetables with a little olive oil and garlic. Please arrive by seven because I would like us to eat (4) ___. If you have time, you can also choose a simple dessert from the bakery (5) ___ the supermarket.\nThanks,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart1_3__fr4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"together"},{"id":"b","text":"spicy"},{"id":"c","text":"late"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI am cooking dinner tonight. Could you buy (1) ___ vegetables on your way home? We already have rice, but there is not (2) ___ milk left for the sauce. I plan to (3) ___ the vegetables with a little olive oil and garlic. Please arrive by seven because I would like us to eat (4) ___. If you have time, you can also choose a simple dessert from the bakery (5) ___ the supermarket.\nThanks,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart1_4__fr5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"beside"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"through"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI am cooking dinner tonight. Could you buy (1) ___ vegetables on your way home? We already have rice, but there is not (2) ___ milk left for the sauce. I plan to (3) ___ the vegetables with a little olive oil and garlic. Please arrive by seven because I would like us to eat (4) ___. If you have time, you can also choose a simple dessert from the bakery (5) ___ the supermarket.\nThanks,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart2_0__fr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I used to order takeaway food several times a week after work.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"When I checked my monthly spending, I realised the habit was costing much more than I expected."},{"id":"s2","text":"I therefore chose three simple meals that used many of the same basic ingredients."},{"id":"s3","text":"On Sunday evening, I prepared vegetables and cooked enough rice for several days."},{"id":"s4","text":"This made weekday cooking faster and also reduced the amount of food I threw away."},{"id":"s5","text":"After a few weeks, cooking at home felt like a normal routine rather than extra work."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart2_1__fr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our community centre decided to organise a small food festival.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"Local restaurants were first invited to offer small tasting portions of their most popular dishes."},{"id":"s2","text":"The organisers then added short cooking demonstrations and activities for children."},{"id":"s3","text":"Because they wanted to reduce waste, vendors were asked to use reusable or compostable materials."},{"id":"s4","text":"On the day of the festival, visitors could try food, learn simple recipes and talk to local producers."},{"id":"s5","text":"The event was so popular that the centre now plans to make it an annual activity."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart4_0__fr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person values control over ingredients."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person sees eating out mainly as a social experience."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person prepares food in advance because of a busy schedule."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person prefers seasonal local products."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person does not think home cooking needs to be complicated."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person uses planning to avoid fast food after work."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person is concerned about packaging and waste."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"c","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I cook most meals at home because I like controlling the ingredients. I am not interested in complicated recipes; simple healthy food is enough for me.\n\nPerson B — I enjoy restaurants because food is part of my social life. Trying unfamiliar dishes with friends is more important to me than saving money every time.\n\nPerson C — My working hours are long, so I prepare several meals at the weekend. Planning ahead stops me from ordering fast food when I get home late.\n\nPerson D — I enjoy shopping at local markets. I like seasonal produce and talking to the people who grow or sell it. I am also trying to reduce packaging and food waste.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T04__rpart5_0__fr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"Why cooking has become a conscious choice"},{"id":"h2","text":"The hidden monthly cost of convenience"},{"id":"h3","text":"Greater control does not automatically mean healthy food"},{"id":"h4","text":"Making cooking possible on busy weekdays"},{"id":"h5","text":"The benefits and limits of cooking together"},{"id":"h6","text":"Why restaurants still have a useful role"},{"id":"h7","text":"Reducing waste through repeated everyday choices"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why professional cooking skills are necessary for every meal"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nCooking at home has changed from being an automatic daily routine into a deliberate choice for many households. Longer working hours, food-delivery apps and inexpensive ready meals make it possible to avoid cooking almost completely. At the same time, interest in nutrition, food prices and environmental impact has encouraged other people to return to the kitchen. The debate is therefore not simply about whether home-cooked food is better. It is about how cooking can fit realistically into modern schedules without becoming another source of pressure.\n\nParagraph B\nCost is one reason people begin preparing more food themselves. A single restaurant or delivery meal may appear affordable, but repeated purchases can create a surprisingly large monthly expense. Home cooking allows ingredients to be used across several meals, particularly when people plan before shopping. Rice, vegetables, eggs or beans can be combined in different ways rather than purchased for only one recipe. The financial advantage becomes greatest when households also reduce food waste instead of allowing unused ingredients to remain in the refrigerator until they must be thrown away.\n\nParagraph C\nControl over ingredients is another attraction. Restaurant meals are not automatically unhealthy, but customers often do not know exactly how much salt, sugar or oil has been used. At home, a person can change quantities and adapt a dish to allergies, preferences or health goals. However, this does not mean every home meal is balanced. People can easily cook food that is high in fat or low in vegetables. The benefit is choice and information rather than a guarantee that food prepared at home will always be healthier.\n\nParagraph D\nTime remains the strongest argument against cooking. After a long working day, even a simple recipe can feel demanding if every ingredient must be washed, chopped and cooked from the beginning. Meal preparation can reduce this problem. Some people prepare several complete meals at the weekend, while others simply wash vegetables, cook grains or prepare sauces in advance. This turns weekday cooking into assembly rather than a long process. The method works best when the prepared ingredients can be combined in different ways, since eating exactly the same meal every day quickly becomes boring.\n\nParagraph E\nCooking can also be social. Families who prepare meals together share tasks and often have conversations that might not happen when one person cooks alone. Children can learn basic skills such as washing vegetables, measuring ingredients or setting the table. These activities may also make them more willing to try unfamiliar food. Of course, shared cooking can become stressful if responsibility is unfairly distributed. Like other household work, it benefits from clear expectations. The positive effect comes from cooperation, not from assuming that one person should produce every meal for everyone else.\n\nParagraph F\nEating out still offers benefits that home cooking cannot fully replace. Restaurants provide convenience, variety and a setting for celebrations or meetings with friends. They also allow people to experience cuisines that require specialised ingredients or techniques. The problem occurs when convenience becomes the default response to every busy evening. Many households find a mixed routine more sustainable: simple home meals on ordinary days and restaurants or takeaway food when time, social plans or special occasions make them useful. Balance is more realistic than treating either option as morally superior.\n\nParagraph G\nThe environmental side of food is receiving increasing attention as well. Household waste includes not only uneaten meals but also ingredients bought without a plan and packaging from frequent deliveries. Better planning, correct storage and creative use of leftovers can reduce this waste. Local and seasonal food may also reduce some transport and packaging, although environmental impact depends on many factors. The broader lesson is similar to the health and financial arguments: small repeated choices matter more than occasional perfect behaviour. A practical food routine should be affordable, enjoyable and sustainable enough to continue.\n\nAdditional note\nCooking habits are also shaped by confidence. People who know only one or two dishes may see the kitchen as a place where mistakes are expensive and stressful. Learning a small number of flexible techniques, such as roasting vegetables, cooking grains and making a basic sauce, can reduce that anxiety. Practical confidence often matters more than collecting complicated recipes that are rarely used.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits"},{"id":"T05__rpart1_0__br1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"more"},{"id":"b","text":"much"},{"id":"c","text":"manyly"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI joined the city library last week because I want to read (1) ___ regularly. The staff showed me how to (2) ___ e-books through the library app. I can keep most digital books for two weeks, (3) ___ I can renew them if nobody is waiting. I also found a quiet study area (4) ___ the second floor. Would you like to come with me (5) ___ Saturday?\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart1_1__br2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"borrow"},{"id":"b","text":"lend"},{"id":"c","text":"owe"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI joined the city library last week because I want to read (1) ___ regularly. The staff showed me how to (2) ___ e-books through the library app. I can keep most digital books for two weeks, (3) ___ I can renew them if nobody is waiting. I also found a quiet study area (4) ___ the second floor. Would you like to come with me (5) ___ Saturday?\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart1_2__br3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"and"},{"id":"b","text":"but of"},{"id":"c","text":"because of"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI joined the city library last week because I want to read (1) ___ regularly. The staff showed me how to (2) ___ e-books through the library app. I can keep most digital books for two weeks, (3) ___ I can renew them if nobody is waiting. I also found a quiet study area (4) ___ the second floor. Would you like to come with me (5) ___ Saturday?\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart1_3__br4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"on"},{"id":"b","text":"at to"},{"id":"c","text":"during"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI joined the city library last week because I want to read (1) ___ regularly. The staff showed me how to (2) ___ e-books through the library app. I can keep most digital books for two weeks, (3) ___ I can renew them if nobody is waiting. I also found a quiet study area (4) ___ the second floor. Would you like to come with me (5) ___ Saturday?\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart1_4__br5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"next"},{"id":"b","text":"last ago"},{"id":"c","text":"during next"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI joined the city library last week because I want to read (1) ___ regularly. The staff showed me how to (2) ___ e-books through the library app. I can keep most digital books for two weeks, (3) ___ I can renew them if nobody is waiting. I also found a quiet study area (4) ___ the second floor. Would you like to come with me (5) ___ Saturday?\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart2_0__br2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I wanted to start reading more regularly, but I often forgot after a busy day.","sentences":[{"id":"s3","text":"I then placed the book on my desk so that I would see it before going to bed."},{"id":"s1","text":"I decided to begin with only ten minutes of reading each evening."},{"id":"s5","text":"After a month, reading had become a normal part of my evening routine."},{"id":"s2","text":"To make the habit easier, I chose a short novel that genuinely interested me."},{"id":"s4","text":"The first few days were difficult, but the small target made it easy to continue."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart2_1__br2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: A dramatic news post appeared in our class group yesterday morning.","sentences":[{"id":"s4","text":"The official website confirmed that the event had happened several years earlier in another country."},{"id":"s2","text":"Instead of forwarding it immediately, I opened the link and checked the publication date."},{"id":"s1","text":"Several students reacted to the headline before anyone had read the full story."},{"id":"s5","text":"We therefore deleted the post from the group and reminded everyone to check sources before sharing."},{"id":"s3","text":"I also searched for the same claim on two reliable news websites."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart4_0__br4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person checks surprising stories using another source."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person chooses paper partly to reduce screen use."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person combines online searching with printed study material."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person limits how frequently they read the news."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person values digital reading while travelling."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person prints material when close study is needed."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person changed a media habit because it affected concentration and feelings."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"c","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I read mostly on my phone because I travel to work by bus. Short articles are convenient, but if a story seems surprising I compare it with another reliable source before I believe it.\n\nPerson B — I prefer printed books for long reading. I spend all day looking at a computer at work, so reading paper in the evening helps me relax and gives my eyes a break.\n\nPerson C — I use both formats. I search academic articles online because it is fast, but I often print the most important pages when I need to study them carefully and make notes.\n\nPerson D — I used to check news constantly. Notifications interrupted my work and sometimes made me anxious, so now I check the news only twice a day and have turned off most alerts.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T05__rpart5_0__br5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"A changing environment around a familiar activity"},{"id":"h2","text":"Removing practical barriers to reading"},{"id":"h3","text":"The need to manage digital distractions"},{"id":"h4","text":"Speed is not the same as deep understanding"},{"id":"h5","text":"New responsibilities when judging information"},{"id":"h6","text":"Readers often use several formats"},{"id":"h7","text":"Choosing a method according to the task"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why printed books will completely disappear"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nFor much of the twentieth century, reading was strongly associated with printed objects. A reader opened a book, magazine or newspaper and moved through pages in a fairly fixed order. Digital devices have not removed that basic activity, but they have changed the environment around it. One phone can now contain novels, textbooks, news reports and reference material, while search tools allow readers to move directly to a word or idea. The important change is therefore not that people have stopped reading, but that reading now happens inside a wider system of links, notifications and digital services.\n\nParagraph B\nThe clearest advantage of digital reading is access. A book can be downloaded within seconds, text can be enlarged, and a built-in dictionary can explain unfamiliar vocabulary immediately. These features are useful for travellers, language learners and people who find small print difficult. Digital libraries can also help students who live far from a campus or cannot visit during normal opening hours. Instead of waiting for a building to open, they can consult many resources from home. The format does not make every book cheaper or easier, but it removes several practical barriers that once limited access.\n\nParagraph C\nConvenience can also create a different kind of attention. On a connected device, a reader may be only one tap away from messages, videos or social media. Even when notifications are turned off, links inside an article make it easy to leave one subject and begin another. This does not prove that screens automatically damage concentration. People can read deeply on a tablet, and they can become distracted while holding a printed book. However, digital reading often requires readers to manage interruptions more actively, especially when the same device is used for work, entertainment and communication.\n\nParagraph D\nResearchers and teachers often distinguish between finding information quickly and building a detailed understanding of a long text. Search functions are excellent when someone needs one fact, one definition or one name. The risk is that a reader begins to treat a document as a collection of separate answers. Complex arguments depend on relationships between earlier and later sections, so sometimes people need to slow down, read in sequence and make notes. This can be done on paper or on a screen. The key skill is recognising when speed is useful and when understanding requires a more deliberate method.\n\nParagraph E\nThe growth of online media has also changed what it means to be a good reader. Understanding vocabulary is no longer enough. A professional-looking page can be created by almost anyone, old photographs can be shared as if they were new, and a quotation can circulate without its original context. Readers therefore need to ask who produced the information, when it appeared, why it was published and whether other reliable sources support the claim. These checks take longer than pressing a share button, but they are increasingly necessary because popularity and accuracy are not the same thing.\n\nParagraph F\nIt is tempting to divide people into two groups: those who love paper and those who prefer screens. Real behaviour is more complicated. The same person may read short news updates on a phone, search academic material on a laptop and keep a printed novel beside the bed. Cost, eyesight, location, habit and purpose all affect the choice. Publishers and libraries have responded by offering the same title in several formats. This mixed behaviour suggests that digital reading and printed reading are not always competitors; they can be tools selected for different situations.\n\nParagraph G\nThe future of reading is therefore unlikely to be decided by one format defeating another. A more useful question is how readers can choose the method that supports a particular task. Schools and libraries can teach traditional comprehension strategies together with digital information skills. Individuals can also improve their own habits by reducing notifications, checking sources and selecting a format that helps them concentrate. Strong readers will probably be those who can change their behaviour deliberately: scanning when speed matters, slowing down when an argument is complex, and verifying information when reliability matters.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media"},{"id":"T06__rpart1_0__tr1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"because"},{"id":"b","text":"although of"},{"id":"c","text":"during"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nI finally bought a new laptop (1) ___ my old one became too slow. I spent yesterday evening (2) ___ the software I need for work. The new computer connects (3) ___ Wi-Fi much faster, and the battery lasts longer (4) ___ before. I still need to move my old files, so I am going to back them (5) ___ tonight.\nSee you,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart1_1__tr2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"installing"},{"id":"b","text":"installed to"},{"id":"c","text":"install"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nI finally bought a new laptop (1) ___ my old one became too slow. I spent yesterday evening (2) ___ the software I need for work. The new computer connects (3) ___ Wi-Fi much faster, and the battery lasts longer (4) ___ before. I still need to move my old files, so I am going to back them (5) ___ tonight.\nSee you,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart1_2__tr3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"to"},{"id":"b","text":"at"},{"id":"c","text":"during"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nI finally bought a new laptop (1) ___ my old one became too slow. I spent yesterday evening (2) ___ the software I need for work. The new computer connects (3) ___ Wi-Fi much faster, and the battery lasts longer (4) ___ before. I still need to move my old files, so I am going to back them (5) ___ tonight.\nSee you,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart1_3__tr4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"than"},{"id":"b","text":"then"},{"id":"c","text":"that"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nI finally bought a new laptop (1) ___ my old one became too slow. I spent yesterday evening (2) ___ the software I need for work. The new computer connects (3) ___ Wi-Fi much faster, and the battery lasts longer (4) ___ before. I still need to move my old files, so I am going to back them (5) ___ tonight.\nSee you,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart1_4__tr5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"up"},{"id":"b","text":"on"},{"id":"c","text":"out of"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Alex,\nI finally bought a new laptop (1) ___ my old one became too slow. I spent yesterday evening (2) ___ the software I need for work. The new computer connects (3) ___ Wi-Fi much faster, and the battery lasts longer (4) ___ before. I still need to move my old files, so I am going to back them (5) ___ tonight.\nSee you,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart2_0__tr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: My laptop became extremely slow whenever I opened several programs.","sentences":[{"id":"s4","text":"After the restart, I opened the same programs again and the computer worked normally."},{"id":"s1","text":"I first checked the storage space and discovered that the main drive was almost full."},{"id":"s5","text":"Since then, I have reviewed old files every month instead of waiting for the problem to return."},{"id":"s2","text":"I deleted temporary files and moved large videos to an external drive."},{"id":"s3","text":"The system then suggested restarting the laptop to complete the clean-up."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart2_1__tr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our department decided to hold more meetings online.","sentences":[{"id":"s2","text":"The IT team then tested two platforms and selected the one that worked best with our existing accounts."},{"id":"s5","text":"After a month, staff reported fewer connection problems and shorter meetings."},{"id":"s1","text":"Before choosing a system, we asked staff which features they needed most."},{"id":"s4","text":"A short training session showed everyone how to join meetings, share screens and mute microphones."},{"id":"s3","text":"Once the decision was made, all employees received instructions for installing the software."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart4_0__tr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person values technology because it reduces travel."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person takes specific steps to protect online accounts."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person combines digital learning with handwritten notes."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person physically separates from a device to improve concentration."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person is careful about where applications come from."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person considers long screen use tiring."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person changed the phone's layout to reduce distraction."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"b","p6":"a","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I work from home two days a week. Online meetings save travel time, but I keep my camera off when it is not needed and take regular breaks because sitting at a screen all day is tiring.\n\nPerson B — I love trying new apps, but I only download them from official stores. I also use different passwords and turn on two-step verification for important accounts.\n\nPerson C — I use technology mainly for study. Videos are useful when a topic is difficult, but I still make handwritten notes because writing helps me remember the main ideas.\n\nPerson D — My phone used to interrupt me constantly. I removed several social-media apps from the home screen and now leave the phone in another room when I need to concentrate.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T06__rpart5_0__tr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h1","text":"Convenience creates dependence as well as benefits"},{"id":"h2","text":"Security through repeatable everyday habits"},{"id":"h3","text":"Why important information needs more than one copy"},{"id":"h4","text":"Communication tools need rules to be productive"},{"id":"h5","text":"Technology should support rather than replace learning"},{"id":"h6","text":"Reducing automatic digital distraction"},{"id":"h7","text":"Digital literacy is broader than operating devices"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why everyone should stay online all day"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nPersonal technology has become ordinary so quickly that many people notice it only when something stops working. Phones provide maps, banking, messages, cameras and access to work systems. Laptops support study, entertainment and professional tasks from the same desk. This concentration of functions creates enormous convenience, but it also means that a technical problem can interrupt several parts of life at once. The question is no longer whether people use digital technology. A more useful question is how they can use it reliably without allowing convenience to create unnecessary risk or distraction.\n\nParagraph B\nSecurity begins with small habits rather than advanced technical knowledge. Reusing one simple password across many services is convenient until one account is compromised. Different strong passwords, password managers and two-step verification reduce that risk. Software updates matter for the same reason: they often fix weaknesses that users cannot see. None of these measures guarantees perfect safety, and overly complicated systems can encourage people to ignore the rules. Good security therefore depends on routines that are strong enough to protect important information but simple enough to follow consistently.\n\nParagraph C\nBackups are another example of preparation that seems unnecessary until something goes wrong. A laptop can fail, a phone can be lost, or a file can be deleted by mistake. Keeping only one copy of important work turns a small accident into a major problem. Cloud storage is convenient because it can synchronise files automatically, while an external drive provides a separate copy that does not depend on the same online account. The best method varies, but the principle is simple: information that matters should not exist in only one place.\n\nParagraph D\nTechnology can also improve how people work together. Video meetings reduce travel, shared documents allow several people to edit the same file, and messaging tools make quick questions easier. Yet adding more communication channels does not automatically improve teamwork. Constant notifications can interrupt focused work, and an online meeting without a clear purpose can waste as much time as a physical one. Productive teams choose tools according to the task and create rules about when immediate responses are necessary. The goal is better coordination, not permanent availability.\n\nParagraph E\nThe same principle applies to learning. Search engines, online courses and artificial-intelligence tools can explain unfamiliar ideas quickly and provide examples at almost any time. The danger is accepting a fast answer without understanding or checking it. A learner may produce polished work while being unable to explain the reasoning independently. Technology is most useful when it supports active learning: asking questions, comparing sources, practising skills and receiving feedback. When it removes every difficulty, it can also remove the part of the task that was meant to develop the learner's ability.\n\nParagraph F\nAttention has become one of the less visible costs of connected devices. Many applications are designed to bring users back through alerts, messages and recommended content. A five-minute check can easily become much longer. Solutions do not require abandoning technology. People can disable unnecessary notifications, keep distracting apps away from the home screen, use focus modes or leave a phone in another room during demanding work. These small changes add useful friction. They make distraction slightly less automatic and give deliberate activities a better chance to continue.\n\nParagraph G\nFuture digital systems will probably become more integrated and more intelligent, but the basic human problems will remain familiar. People will still need to decide which information to trust, which tasks deserve attention and which risks require protection. Digital literacy therefore includes more than knowing how to operate a device. It includes security, judgement, communication habits and the ability to choose when technology is helpful and when a simpler method is better. The most capable users may not be those who use the most technology, but those who use it deliberately.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet"},{"id":"T07__rpart1_0__sr1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"because"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"although of"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI recently joined a local community club (1) ___ I wanted to meet more people in my area. The members organise activities (2) ___ weekends, including walks, games and charity events. Last Saturday I helped (3) ___ at a food collection. Everyone was very friendly, (4) ___ I felt comfortable quickly. Would you like to come (5) ___ me next time?\nBest,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart1_1__sr2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"by"},{"id":"b","text":"to"},{"id":"c","text":"at"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Anna,\nI recently joined a local community club (1) ___ I wanted to meet more people in my area. The members organise activities (2) ___ weekends, including walks, games and charity events. Last Saturday I helped (3) ___ at a food collection. Everyone was very friendly, (4) ___ I felt comfortable quickly. Would you like to come (5) ___ me next time?\nBest,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart1_2__sr3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"over"},{"id":"b","text":"out"},{"id":"c","text":"off"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Anna,\nI recently joined a local community club (1) ___ I wanted to meet more people in my area. The members organise activities (2) ___ weekends, including walks, games and charity events. Last Saturday I helped (3) ___ at a food collection. Everyone was very friendly, (4) ___ I felt comfortable quickly. Would you like to come (5) ___ me next time?\nBest,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart1_3__sr4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"despite"},{"id":"b","text":"because of"},{"id":"c","text":"so"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Anna,\nI recently joined a local community club (1) ___ I wanted to meet more people in my area. The members organise activities (2) ___ weekends, including walks, games and charity events. Last Saturday I helped (3) ___ at a food collection. Everyone was very friendly, (4) ___ I felt comfortable quickly. Would you like to come (5) ___ me next time?\nBest,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart1_4__sr5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"with"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"from"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Anna,\nI recently joined a local community club (1) ___ I wanted to meet more people in my area. The members organise activities (2) ___ weekends, including walks, games and charity events. Last Saturday I helped (3) ___ at a food collection. Everyone was very friendly, (4) ___ I felt comfortable quickly. Would you like to come (5) ___ me next time?\nBest,\nMia","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart2_0__sr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: When I moved to a new neighbourhood, I did not know anyone nearby.","sentences":[{"id":"s5","text":"After a few months, some of those people had become good friends."},{"id":"s1","text":"A week later, I noticed a poster for a local walking club in the community centre."},{"id":"s3","text":"At the first meeting, another member introduced me to several people with similar interests."},{"id":"s4","text":"We talked during the walk and arranged to meet again for coffee the following week."},{"id":"s2","text":"I sent the organiser a message and decided to attend the next Saturday walk."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart2_1__sr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our residents' group wanted to make the small park more useful for families.","sentences":[{"id":"s3","text":"After discussing the cost, the group chose several improvements that volunteers could complete themselves."},{"id":"s1","text":"We first asked local people what changes they would like to see."},{"id":"s5","text":"The improvement day was successful, so the group decided to organise another one in autumn."},{"id":"s4","text":"On Saturday morning, volunteers planted flowers, painted benches and collected rubbish."},{"id":"s2","text":"The most popular ideas were more seating, better lighting and a small play area."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart4_0__sr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person values a club mainly because of regular social contact."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person uses online communication for long-distance relationships."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person has gained experience from people with different backgrounds."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person prefers small social situations."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person believes online contact cannot fully replace face-to-face meetings."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person originally joined an activity after moving somewhere new."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person helps the community on a regular schedule."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"},{"id":"a","text":"Person A"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"b","p6":"a","p7":"c"},"context":"Person A — I joined a running club after moving to a new city. The exercise is useful, but the biggest benefit is having regular contact with people who now feel like friends.\n\nPerson B — I like social media for staying in touch with relatives who live far away. However, I do not think online messages can completely replace meeting someone face to face.\n\nPerson C — I volunteer at a local food bank twice a month. I started because I wanted to help, but I have also learned a lot from working with people of different ages and backgrounds.\n\nPerson D — Large social events make me tired, so I prefer smaller groups. I enjoy meeting two or three friends for coffee because conversations are easier and more personal.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T07__rpart5_0__sr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h8","text":"Why everyone should attend large parties"},{"id":"h4","text":"Online contact is useful but has limits"},{"id":"h7","text":"Small social connections can still matter"},{"id":"h2","text":"Regular contact can help relationships develop"},{"id":"h6","text":"Keeping members involved requires more than invitations"},{"id":"h3","text":"Helping others can also create social benefits"},{"id":"h1","text":"Community now has several different forms"},{"id":"h5","text":"Different people need different kinds of social activity"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nPeople often think of community as something defined by geography: neighbours living on the same street or families using the same local services. That remains important, but modern communities can also form around interests, work, study, hobbies or online activities. A person may belong to several groups at the same time. These communities provide different kinds of contact, from practical help between neighbours to friendships based on shared interests. The word therefore describes not one fixed structure but a network of relationships that can develop in many settings.\n\nParagraph B\nOne reason local groups remain valuable is that repeated contact makes relationships easier to build. A single conversation at a large event may be enjoyable but quickly forgotten. Meeting the same people every week at a sports club, language exchange or volunteer activity gives them time to learn about one another gradually. Familiarity can reduce the awkwardness of starting conversations, especially for newcomers. Friendship is not guaranteed, but regular activities create conditions in which trust and connection are more likely to grow.\n\nParagraph C\nVolunteering adds another dimension because the activity is organised around a shared purpose. People may collect food, clean a public area, support an event or help older residents. The immediate goal is useful work, yet volunteers often report social benefits as well. They meet people outside their usual circle, practise teamwork and feel more connected to the place where they live. The strongest programmes are usually clear about what help is needed, so volunteers understand their role instead of simply being invited to “do something good”.\n\nParagraph D\nDigital communication has expanded the meaning of staying in touch. Families can speak across countries, old classmates can reconnect and people with unusual interests can find others like them. These benefits are real, particularly when distance makes physical meetings difficult. The weakness is that frequent messages do not always create deep relationships. A person can receive many updates while still feeling isolated. Online contact works best when it supports genuine conversation and, where possible, is combined with richer forms of interaction.\n\nParagraph E\nSocial activities are not equally comfortable for everyone. Large parties may energise one person and exhaust another. Some people prefer structured activities because a shared task gives them something to talk about, while others are comfortable entering a room of strangers. Successful clubs recognise these differences. They may provide small-group activities, clear introductions and opportunities to join without immediately speaking in front of everyone. Making participation easier is not the same as forcing people to be highly social.\n\nParagraph F\nCommunity groups also face a practical problem: attracting members is easier than keeping them involved. People join with good intentions but may stop attending when work becomes busy or activities feel repetitive. Successful organisers therefore communicate clearly, vary the programme and give members small responsibilities. A person who helps plan one event or welcomes new members may feel more committed than someone who only receives announcements. Participation grows stronger when members believe the group needs their contribution.\n\nParagraph G\nStrong communities do not require everyone to become close friends. Their value can also come from weaker connections: knowing a neighbour's name, recognising people at a local café or having someone to ask for simple information. These small links make places feel more familiar and can provide practical support when needed. The goal is therefore not maximum social activity. It is creating enough opportunities for people to connect in ways that suit their lives, personalities and interests.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends"},{"id":"T08__rpart1_0__wr1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"during"},{"id":"b","text":"at"},{"id":"c","text":"on"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Ben,\nI started a new job (1) ___ Monday, and the first week has been busy. My office is close (2) ___ the station, so travelling is easy. I usually start at eight thirty (3) ___ finish at five. Yesterday my manager asked me (4) ___ prepare a short report for Friday. I am still learning the system, but my colleagues have been very helpful (5) ___ me.\nBest,\nAlex","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart1_1__wr2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"to"},{"id":"b","text":"with"},{"id":"c","text":"from"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nI started a new job (1) ___ Monday, and the first week has been busy. My office is close (2) ___ the station, so travelling is easy. I usually start at eight thirty (3) ___ finish at five. Yesterday my manager asked me (4) ___ prepare a short report for Friday. I am still learning the system, but my colleagues have been very helpful (5) ___ me.\nBest,\nAlex","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart1_2__wr3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"and"},{"id":"b","text":"because of"},{"id":"c","text":"but of"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nI started a new job (1) ___ Monday, and the first week has been busy. My office is close (2) ___ the station, so travelling is easy. I usually start at eight thirty (3) ___ finish at five. Yesterday my manager asked me (4) ___ prepare a short report for Friday. I am still learning the system, but my colleagues have been very helpful (5) ___ me.\nBest,\nAlex","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart1_3__wr4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"that I"},{"id":"b","text":"to"},{"id":"c","text":"for to"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Ben,\nI started a new job (1) ___ Monday, and the first week has been busy. My office is close (2) ___ the station, so travelling is easy. I usually start at eight thirty (3) ___ finish at five. Yesterday my manager asked me (4) ___ prepare a short report for Friday. I am still learning the system, but my colleagues have been very helpful (5) ___ me.\nBest,\nAlex","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart1_4__wr5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"during"},{"id":"b","text":"at"},{"id":"c","text":"to"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Ben,\nI started a new job (1) ___ Monday, and the first week has been busy. My office is close (2) ___ the station, so travelling is easy. I usually start at eight thirty (3) ___ finish at five. Yesterday my manager asked me (4) ___ prepare a short report for Friday. I am still learning the system, but my colleagues have been very helpful (5) ___ me.\nBest,\nAlex","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart2_0__wr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I recently ordered a pair of shoes from an online store.","sentences":[{"id":"s2","text":"I checked the return policy and contacted the store through its website."},{"id":"s3","text":"The customer service team replied the same afternoon and sent me a free return label."},{"id":"s4","text":"I packed the original shoes, attached the label and returned them the next morning."},{"id":"s5","text":"Two days later, the correct size arrived and I was satisfied with how the problem was handled."},{"id":"s1","text":"When the parcel arrived, I discovered that the shoes were the wrong size."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart2_1__wr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our team was asked to prepare a presentation for an important client.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"We first met to agree on the main message and the information the client needed."},{"id":"s4","text":"The day before the meeting, we practised the presentation and shortened two sections."},{"id":"s3","text":"We shared our drafts in one document so everyone could review the full presentation."},{"id":"s2","text":"After that, each person was given responsibility for one part of the presentation."},{"id":"s5","text":"The client meeting went well, and the team received positive feedback."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart4_0__wr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person chooses home working for focused tasks."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person uses different shopping methods for different products."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person changed employment mainly to develop skills."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person believes online sellers need to build customer trust."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person still values the office for discussion."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person likes the speed of comparing prices online."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person operates a small business."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"b","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I prefer working from home for tasks that require concentration because there are fewer interruptions. I still go to the office for meetings where discussion is important.\n\nPerson B — I usually shop online because comparing prices is fast. However, I buy clothes in a store because I want to check the size and material before paying.\n\nPerson C — I recently changed jobs because I wanted more opportunities to learn. A higher salary was useful, but professional development was the main reason.\n\nPerson D — I run a small online business. Good customer service matters because people cannot examine the product in person, so clear descriptions and quick replies help build trust.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T08__rpart5_0__wr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h2","text":"Flexible work requires deliberate communication"},{"id":"h1","text":"What workers expect from a job is becoming broader"},{"id":"h6","text":"Reviews are useful but should be interpreted carefully"},{"id":"h5","text":"Trust and service encourage customers to return"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why price is the only factor that matters"},{"id":"h3","text":"Learning opportunities can influence job decisions"},{"id":"h7","text":"Technology will change tasks but not remove every human role"},{"id":"h4","text":"Shoppers often combine online and physical channels"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nThe idea of a normal working day has changed for many employees. Some people still travel to the same workplace every morning, while others divide their week between home and an office. Digital tools make this flexibility possible, but location is only one part of the change. Employees increasingly judge jobs by a combination of salary, working hours, opportunities to learn, management style and personal flexibility. Employers therefore compete not only through pay but through the overall experience they offer.\n\nParagraph B\nRemote work can improve concentration when home provides a quiet environment, and it can remove time spent travelling. Its weaknesses become clear when communication is poor. A short question that would take two minutes in an office may develop into a long series of messages. Teams therefore need clear rules about which tasks require a meeting, which can be handled in writing and when people are expected to respond. Flexibility works best when communication becomes more deliberate rather than simply more frequent.\n\nParagraph C\nProfessional development has also become an important reason for changing jobs. A worker may accept a role with a similar salary if it provides better training, stronger mentoring or experience with new technology. This matters to employers because replacing skilled staff is expensive. Companies that want to keep people often need to show how a role can develop over time. A promotion is one path, but new responsibilities, useful courses and opportunities to work on different projects can also keep employees interested.\n\nParagraph D\nShopping habits show a similar pattern of combining old and new methods. Online stores make it easy to compare prices, read reviews and order at any time. Physical shops offer something different: customers can touch products, try on clothes and receive immediate help. Many shoppers use both channels during one purchase. They may research online, examine the product in a store and then buy wherever the combination of price, convenience and service is strongest.\n\nParagraph E\nFor businesses, price remains important, but customer experience can influence whether buyers return. A cheap product is less attractive if delivery information is unclear or complaints are ignored. Online businesses have a particular challenge because customers may never meet the seller. Accurate descriptions, realistic photographs, clear return policies and quick replies reduce uncertainty. Trust is valuable because a satisfied customer may return without repeating the full process of comparing many competing sellers.\n\nParagraph F\nReviews have become powerful because they reduce the information gap between a business and a new customer. They can reveal common problems or confirm that a service is reliable. Reviews are not perfect evidence. Some may be unusually positive, unfairly negative or even false. Sensible shoppers therefore look for patterns across several comments instead of allowing one dramatic review to decide everything. Businesses also learn from recurring complaints because repeated criticism may identify a real weakness.\n\nParagraph G\nThe boundary between work, business and shopping will probably continue to change as digital services become more integrated. Artificial intelligence may handle routine customer questions, recommend products or help employees prepare information. The important issue is not simply whether a task can be automated. Businesses must decide where technology improves speed and where human judgement, trust or personal contact still adds value. The strongest organisations will use new tools without assuming that convenience is the only thing customers or workers care about.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping"},{"id":"T09__rpart1_0__er1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"at"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"on"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Sam,\nI started an evening English course (1) ___ Monday. The class meets twice (2) ___ week, and each lesson lasts ninety minutes. Our teacher gives us short exercises to complete (3) ___ home. I joined the course (4) ___ I want to improve my speaking before an important exam. So far, I am enjoying it (5) ___ much.\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart1_1__er2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"a"},{"id":"b","text":"by"},{"id":"c","text":"the"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI started an evening English course (1) ___ Monday. The class meets twice (2) ___ week, and each lesson lasts ninety minutes. Our teacher gives us short exercises to complete (3) ___ home. I joined the course (4) ___ I want to improve my speaking before an important exam. So far, I am enjoying it (5) ___ much.\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart1_2__er3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"from"},{"id":"b","text":"to"},{"id":"c","text":"at"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Sam,\nI started an evening English course (1) ___ Monday. The class meets twice (2) ___ week, and each lesson lasts ninety minutes. Our teacher gives us short exercises to complete (3) ___ home. I joined the course (4) ___ I want to improve my speaking before an important exam. So far, I am enjoying it (5) ___ much.\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart1_3__er4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"because"},{"id":"b","text":"during"},{"id":"c","text":"although of"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Sam,\nI started an evening English course (1) ___ Monday. The class meets twice (2) ___ week, and each lesson lasts ninety minutes. Our teacher gives us short exercises to complete (3) ___ home. I joined the course (4) ___ I want to improve my speaking before an important exam. So far, I am enjoying it (5) ___ much.\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart1_4__er5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"too of"},{"id":"b","text":"very"},{"id":"c","text":"many"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Sam,\nI started an evening English course (1) ___ Monday. The class meets twice (2) ___ week, and each lesson lasts ninety minutes. Our teacher gives us short exercises to complete (3) ___ home. I joined the course (4) ___ I want to improve my speaking before an important exam. So far, I am enjoying it (5) ___ much.\nBest,\nNina","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart2_0__er2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I decided to improve my English before applying for a new job.","sentences":[{"id":"s1","text":"I first checked what level I needed and which skills were most important."},{"id":"s2","text":"Then I searched for courses that focused mainly on speaking and workplace communication."},{"id":"s4","text":"I attended every lesson and practised with a classmate between classes."},{"id":"s5","text":"After two months, I felt more confident using English at work."},{"id":"s3","text":"After comparing several options, I chose a short evening course near my office."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart2_1__er2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: Our teacher asked us to prepare a group presentation.","sentences":[{"id":"s4","text":"The day before the presentation, we practised together and shortened two sections."},{"id":"s2","text":"We then divided the topic into three parts so each person had a clear responsibility."},{"id":"s5","text":"The presentation went well, and the teacher gave us useful feedback."},{"id":"s1","text":"First, we met after class and agreed on the main message."},{"id":"s3","text":"Each student researched one part and added their notes to a shared document."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart4_0__er4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person values immediate interaction with a teacher."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person needs flexible study times."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person prefers learning through practice."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person changed from long study sessions to a daily routine."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person benefits from other learners' ideas."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person may study very late."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person believes active practice helps memory."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"},{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"c","text":"Person C"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"b","p7":"c"},"context":"Person A — I prefer classroom courses because I can ask questions immediately and learn from other students' comments.\n\nPerson B — I use online courses because I work irregular hours. I like being able to study when I have time, even late at night.\n\nPerson C — I learn best by doing something practical. Videos and explanations help, but I remember more when I practise the skill myself.\n\nPerson D — I used to study for hours before exams, but now I review for a short time every day. The smaller routine is easier to maintain.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T09__rpart5_0__er5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h6","text":"Real learning requires active recall and use"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why examinations should disappear"},{"id":"h2","text":"Small regular study can outperform last-minute effort"},{"id":"h4","text":"Flexibility can create problems with delay"},{"id":"h7","text":"Good learners combine methods according to purpose"},{"id":"h3","text":"Useful feedback must lead to action"},{"id":"h5","text":"Face-to-face learning offers immediate interaction"},{"id":"h1","text":"More learning choices also require better judgement"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nLearning no longer happens only in classrooms. People study through universities, short courses, workplace training, videos, apps and online communities. The variety is useful because learners can choose methods that fit their goals, schedules and budgets. However, more choice also creates a new responsibility: learners must decide which resources are reliable and which methods actually help them improve.\n\nParagraph B\nRegular practice is one of the strongest predictors of progress. A student who studies for fifteen minutes every day may remember more than someone who studies for several hours only before an exam. Small routines reduce the effort needed to begin. Once a habit is connected to a regular time or place, it becomes easier to continue even during busy weeks.\n\nParagraph C\nFeedback is another important part of learning. Without it, learners may repeat the same mistake for a long time. Good feedback is specific: it explains what is working, what needs improvement and what the learner should try next. Feedback can come from a teacher, a classmate, software or self-review, but it is most useful when the learner does something with it.\n\nParagraph D\nOnline learning has clear advantages. It reduces travel, gives access to courses from other places and allows people to study at convenient times. Its weakness is that flexibility can become delay. When there is no fixed classroom, some learners keep postponing lessons. Successful online students therefore create deadlines, study schedules and a regular place for learning.\n\nParagraph E\nClassroom learning offers a different benefit: immediate human interaction. Students can ask a question, notice when others are confused and participate in spontaneous discussion. These moments are difficult to reproduce perfectly online. On the other hand, a classroom requires everyone to be in the same place at the same time, which is not always practical.\n\nParagraph F\nEffective learners also know that understanding is different from recognition. Reading a page or watching a video can feel easy because the information is visible. Real learning becomes clearer when the material is removed and the learner tries to explain, recall or use it. Practice tests, summaries and teaching someone else are useful because they require active retrieval.\n\nParagraph G\nThe best learning method is therefore not the same for every person or every task. A language learner may combine classroom conversation, online vocabulary practice and independent reading. A professional may use videos for theory and workplace practice for real skills. Strong learners choose methods deliberately and adjust them when progress is slow.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses"},{"id":"T10__rpart1_0__hr1","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 1","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"over"},{"id":"b","text":"at"},{"id":"c","text":"from"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nI have changed my daily routine (1) ___ the last few months. I now walk to work three days (2) ___ week and prepare lunch at home. I also try to go to bed (3) ___ midnight because I feel better when I sleep enough. These small changes are simple, (4) ___ they have given me more energy. Would you like to go (5) ___ a walk this weekend?\nBest,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart1_1__hr2","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 2","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"by"},{"id":"b","text":"the"},{"id":"c","text":"a"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Ben,\nI have changed my daily routine (1) ___ the last few months. I now walk to work three days (2) ___ week and prepare lunch at home. I also try to go to bed (3) ___ midnight because I feel better when I sleep enough. These small changes are simple, (4) ___ they have given me more energy. Would you like to go (5) ___ a walk this weekend?\nBest,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart1_2__hr3","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 3","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"before"},{"id":"b","text":"with"},{"id":"c","text":"during"}],"answer":"a","context":"Hi Ben,\nI have changed my daily routine (1) ___ the last few months. I now walk to work three days (2) ___ week and prepare lunch at home. I also try to go to bed (3) ___ midnight because I feel better when I sleep enough. These small changes are simple, (4) ___ they have given me more energy. Would you like to go (5) ___ a walk this weekend?\nBest,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart1_3__hr4","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 4","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"despite"},{"id":"b","text":"because of"},{"id":"c","text":"but"}],"answer":"c","context":"Hi Ben,\nI have changed my daily routine (1) ___ the last few months. I now walk to work three days (2) ___ week and prepare lunch at home. I also try to go to bed (3) ___ midnight because I feel better when I sleep enough. These small changes are simple, (4) ___ they have given me more energy. Would you like to go (5) ___ a walk this weekend?\nBest,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart1_4__hr5","type":"multipleChoice","prompt":"Gap 5","choices":[{"id":"a","text":"during"},{"id":"b","text":"for"},{"id":"c","text":"to"}],"answer":"b","context":"Hi Ben,\nI have changed my daily routine (1) ___ the last few months. I now walk to work three days (2) ___ week and prepare lunch at home. I also try to go to bed (3) ___ midnight because I feel better when I sleep enough. These small changes are simple, (4) ___ they have given me more energy. Would you like to go (5) ___ a walk this weekend?\nBest,\nTom","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part1","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart2_0__hr2a","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task A — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I wanted to become more active after spending most of the day sitting at a desk.","sentences":[{"id":"s4","text":"After two weeks, the walk had become part of my normal evening routine."},{"id":"s2","text":"To make it easier, I left my walking shoes beside the door."},{"id":"s1","text":"I decided to begin with a twenty-minute walk after dinner."},{"id":"s5","text":"I then increased the distance slightly because I was enjoying the habit."},{"id":"s3","text":"The first few days felt difficult, but the small target was manageable."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart2_1__hr2b","type":"reorder","prompt":"Task B — Put the five sentences in order.","context":"Fixed first sentence: I realised that I was eating takeaway food too often.","sentences":[{"id":"s5","text":"After a month, I was spending less money and eating more vegetables."},{"id":"s2","text":"Then I chose three easy recipes that took less than thirty minutes."},{"id":"s1","text":"I first wrote down the meals I usually bought during busy workdays."},{"id":"s4","text":"During the week, I only needed to heat the food or add a fresh salad."},{"id":"s3","text":"On Sunday, I prepared two simple meals and stored them in the fridge."}],"answer":["s1","s2","s3","s4","s5"],"originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part2","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart4_0__hr4m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match each statement to Person A, B, C or D.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"This person exercises before work."},{"id":"p2","text":"This person changed a phone habit to improve sleep."},{"id":"p3","text":"This person cooks partly to control food choices."},{"id":"p4","text":"This person stays active without using a gym."},{"id":"p5","text":"This person avoids evening exercise because work may interfere."},{"id":"p6","text":"This person saves money by preparing food."},{"id":"p7","text":"This person uses everyday movement as exercise."}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"c","text":"Person C"},{"id":"b","text":"Person B"},{"id":"a","text":"Person A"},{"id":"d","text":"Person D"}],"answer":{"p1":"a","p2":"b","p3":"c","p4":"d","p5":"a","p6":"c","p7":"d"},"context":"Person A — I exercise early in the morning because if I wait until evening, work often becomes too busy.\n\nPerson B — I focus mainly on sleep. I stopped checking my phone in bed, and I now feel more alert in the morning.\n\nPerson C — I prepare meals at home because it helps me control what I eat and spend less money.\n\nPerson D — I do not enjoy gyms, so I stay active by walking to work and taking the stairs whenever possible.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part4","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"},{"id":"T10__rpart5_0__hr5m","type":"matching","prompt":"Match Paragraphs A–G with the best heading.","matchPrompts":[{"id":"p1","text":"Paragraph A"},{"id":"p2","text":"Paragraph B"},{"id":"p3","text":"Paragraph C"},{"id":"p4","text":"Paragraph D"},{"id":"p5","text":"Paragraph E"},{"id":"p6","text":"Paragraph F"},{"id":"p7","text":"Paragraph G"}],"matchOptions":[{"id":"h4","text":"Food choices are influenced by preparation and environment"},{"id":"h6","text":"Technology can help or harm healthy habits"},{"id":"h3","text":"Sleep needs a supportive routine"},{"id":"h2","text":"Everyday movement can contribute to activity"},{"id":"h8","text":"Why everyone should follow the same strict plan"},{"id":"h7","text":"Healthy routines should fit individual lives"},{"id":"h5","text":"Recovery matters when managing stress"},{"id":"h1","text":"Small changes may be easier to maintain"}],"answer":{"p1":"h1","p2":"h2","p3":"h3","p4":"h4","p5":"h5","p6":"h6","p7":"h7"},"context":"Paragraph A\nPeople often make lifestyle changes by setting large goals: exercise every day, stop eating unhealthy food or sleep eight hours immediately. These goals sound positive, but they can be difficult to maintain. Small changes are less impressive, yet they may be more realistic. Walking for fifteen minutes or preparing one healthy meal is easier to begin, and repeated actions can gradually become part of normal life.\n\nParagraph B\nPhysical activity does not need to happen only in a gym. Walking to work, taking the stairs, cycling short distances and doing household tasks all use energy. Structured exercise is still useful because it can improve fitness in a planned way, but everyday movement also matters. People who dislike gyms may be more consistent when activity is connected to their normal routine.\n\nParagraph C\nSleep is often treated as time that can be reduced when life becomes busy. In reality, poor sleep can affect concentration, mood and energy. A regular sleep schedule can help, as can reducing bright screens, caffeine and heavy meals late at night. The goal is not perfect sleep every night but a routine that gives the body a reliable chance to rest.\n\nParagraph D\nHealthy eating is also easier when the environment supports it. If convenient food at home is mainly fruit, vegetables and simple meals, people are more likely to choose them when they are tired. Meal preparation can reduce dependence on takeaway food. It also makes portions and ingredients easier to control, although it requires some planning.\n\nParagraph E\nStress management is not simply about avoiding difficult situations. Work, family and study will continue to create pressure. The useful question is how people recover. Exercise, sleep, social contact, hobbies and short breaks can all help. Different people need different methods, so stress management works best when individuals notice what actually helps them feel calmer and more focused.\n\nParagraph F\nTechnology can support healthy habits but can also interfere with them. Fitness apps can track activity and reminders can encourage movement. On the other hand, phones can reduce sleep and concentration when notifications continue late into the evening. Technology is therefore most useful when it supports a clear goal instead of creating another source of distraction.\n\nParagraph G\nA healthy lifestyle is not one perfect routine that everyone should copy. Work schedules, age, family responsibilities and personal preferences all matter. The strongest approach is flexible: choose habits that are realistic, notice whether they improve energy and wellbeing, and adjust them when life changes. Consistency is usually more important than perfection.","originSkill":"reading","originPart":"part5","topic":"Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"}],"topics":["Day 1 · Family & Home","Day 1 · Travel & Transport","Day 2 · Sports & Outdoor Activities","Day 2 · Food, Cooking & Eating Habits","Day 3 · Books, Reading & Media","Day 3 · Technology, Computer & Internet","Day 4 · Social, Community & Friends","Day 4 · Work, Business & Shopping","Day 5 · Education, Learning & Courses","Day 5 · Health, Lifestyle & Daily Habits"]};
